<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinn Féin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinn Féin is an Irish republican political party founded by Arthur Griffith in 1905. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> it became the main nationalist party, winning most Irish seats in 1918 and setting up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a>. It split over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a>, and later revivals of the name became linked with the Irish Republican Army; it is now a major party in both parts of Ireland.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Easter Rising in Dublin in 1916 was quickly suppressed, but the execution of its leaders shifted opinion towards <b>Sinn Féin</b>, which won 73 of Ireland’s 105 seats in the general election of December 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Already famous for his account of the Indian Rebellion of 1857, he studied in London between 1906 and 1910, where he worked out his idea of Hindu identity and befriended Indian student groups and others such as <b>Sinn Féin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>During the War of Independence it was called the &quot;<b>Sinn Féin</b> anthem&quot;, and British forces confiscated copies as seditious; Éamon de Valera's platform at the East Clare by-election of June 1917 carried its opening lines on a banner.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amhran-na-bhfiann">Amhrán na bhFiann</a></li><li><blockquote>At a convention in Dublin, Arthur Griffith set out the <b>Sinn Féin</b> policy, meaning 'we ourselves', of Irish self-reliance and a separate Irish parliament.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Sinn Fein</b> won most Irish seats in the 1918 general election, its members met in Dublin on 21 January 1919 as Dail Eireann, adopted a Declaration of Independence and created a government, with Eamon de Valera as president from April 1919.</blockquote><a href="/state/irish-republic">Irish Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sinn Fein</b> won 124 of the 128 seats unopposed but treated the election as one for the second Dail of the Irish Republic, so the Parliament of Southern Ireland met only twice, in June 1921 and January 1922.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ireland">Southern Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
